add('sl', 119, 163, 'Matthew Henry', 'Salmos 119:163 — Matthew Henry: Love and hatred are the leading affections of the soul; if those be fixed aright, the rest move accordingly. Here we have them fixed aright...', 'teologico', 'resumo');
add('sl', 119, 164, 'Matthew Henry', 'David, in this psalm, is full of complaints, yet those did neither jostle out his praises nor put him out of tune for them; whatever condition a child of God is in he does not want matter for praise and therefore should not want a heart. See here, 1. How often David praised God - Seven times a day, that is, very frequently, not only every day, but often every day. Many think that once a week will serve, or once or twice a day, but David would praise God seven times a day at least. Praising Go...', 'teologico', 'resumo');
add('sl', 119, 165, 'Matthew Henry', 'Salmos 119:165 — Matthew Henry: Here is an account of the happiness of good men, who are governed by a principle of love to the word of Deus, who make...', 'teologico', 'resumo');
add('sl', 119, 166, 'Matthew Henry', 'Salmos 119:166 — Matthew Henry: Here is the whole duty of man; for we are taught, 1. To keep our eye upon Deus s favour as our end: "Lord, I...', 'teologico', 'resumo');
add('sl', 119, 167, 'Matthew Henry', 'David\'s conscience here witnesses for him,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nI. That his practices were good. 1. He loved God\'s testimonies, he loved them exceedingly. Our love to the word of God must be a superlative love (we must love it better than the wealth and pleasure of this world), and it must be a victorious love, such as will subdue and mortify our lusts and extirpate carnal affections. 2. He kept them, his soul kept them. Bodily exercise profits little in religion; we must make heart-work of it or we make nothing ...', 'teologico', 'resumo');
add('sl', 119, 169, 'Matthew Henry', 'Salmos 119:169 — Matthew Henry: Here we have, I. A general petition for audience repeated: Let my cry come near before thee; and again, Let my supplication come before thee....', 'teologico', 'resumo');
add('sl', 119, 171, 'Matthew Henry', 'Salmos 119:171 — Matthew Henry: Here is, 1. A great favour which David expects from Deus, that he will teach him his statutes. This he had often prayed for in...', 'teologico', 'resumo');
add('sl', 119, 172, 'Matthew Henry', 'Salmos 119:172 — Matthew Henry: Observe here, 1. The good knowledge David had of the word of Deus; he knew it so well that he was ready to own, with...', 'teologico', 'resumo');
add('sl', 119, 173, 'Matthew Henry', 'Salmos 119:173 — Matthew Henry: Here, 1. David prays that divine grace would work for him: Let thy hand help me. He finds his own hands are not sufficient for...', 'teologico', 'resumo');
add('sl', 119, 175, 'Matthew Henry', 'Salmos 119:175 — Matthew Henry: David s heart is still upon praising Deus; and therefore, 1. He prays that Deus would give him time to praise him: "Let my soul...', 'teologico', 'resumo');
add('sl', 119, 176, 'Matthew Henry', 'Here is, 1. A penitent confession: I have gone astray, or wander up and down, like a lost sheep. As unconverted sinners are like lost sheep (Luk 15:4), so weak unsteady saints are like lost sheep, Mat 18:12, Mat 18:13. We are apt to wander like sheep, and very unapt, when we have gone astray, to find the way again. By going astray we lose the comfort of the green pastures and expose ourselves to a thousand mischiefs. 2. A believing petition: Seek thy servant, as the good shepherd seeks a wand...', 'teologico', 'resumo');
